Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept in this agreement subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Segment:
"Company Material Adverse Effect" means any event, occurrence, condition, or change, whether on its own or in the aggregate with other effects, that has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse impact on the business, assets, liabilities, condition, or operations of the Company. In particular, any event that would prevent, delay, or materially impair the Company’s ability to consummate the Transaction shall, prima facie, be considered a Material Adverse Effect. However, notwithstanding the foregoing, for purposes of determining whether such an effect exists, the following shall be excluded:
  (i) any adverse effect arising from general economic changes or fluctuations in financial markets where such changes are common to all competitors within the Company’s industry;
  (ii) any regulatory or legislative modifications of a general nature affecting all companies in the sector, even if they delay or impair consummation;
  (iii) any events that solely affect market sentiment, including transient declines in share price or trading volumes;
  (iv) any conditions that, while impairing the ability to consummate, are demonstrably not unique to the Company and mirror industry-wide impacts.
Accordingly, if the impairment of the ability to consummate is due to any of the above-listed exclusions, it shall not be treated as establishing a Company Material Adverse Effect under this Agreement.

