Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt: 
“Material Adverse Condition” shall mean any occurrence, event, change or circumstance (each, an “Occurrence”) that, either individually or when combined with other Occurrences, (a) has or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries or (b) would materially delay or impair the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following Occurrences shall be excepted and shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Condition:
   (i) changes in general economic conditions or disruptions in international financial markets;
   (ii) fluctuations in the Company’s share price or normal market trading volumes, provided such fluctuations do not have a disproportionate adverse effect relative to industry comparators;
   (iii) any effect arising solely from routine operational adjustments, seasonal business fluctuations, or standard revisions of GAAP occurring after the Agreement date;
   (iv) adverse impacts solely attributable to the public disclosure of this Agreement or related transactions, absent any disproportionate operational or regulatory detriment.
For the purposes of this definition, if an Occurrence both impairs the ability to consummate the transactions and falls within any of the exceptions above, it shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Condition.

Does the foregoing definition imply that the ability to consummate the transactions is subject to MAE carveouts?
