Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a proposed Acquisition Agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'capability to consummate' provision subject to exceptions (MAE carveouts)?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Agreement Excerpt:
"Acquisition Impact" shall mean any occurrence, event, change, or development that, either individually or in aggregate with all other occurrences existing up to the determination date, (1) adversely affects the overall business, financial status, or operational performance of the Acquired Entity, or (2) would reasonably be expected to prevent, delay materially, or hamper the Acquired Entity's capability to consummate the Acquisition; but, with respect to subclause (1) only, none of the following shall be deemed to constitute an Acquisition Impact or be considered when evaluating such impact:
   (i) variances due solely to fluctuations in macroeconomic conditions, including general shifts in the global or domestic economic climate;
   (ii) changes in the regulatory environment, in the absence of a disproportional impact on the Acquired Entity relative to its industry peers;
   (iii) market-wide changes in interest rates and currency exchange rates, except where such changes disproportionately impair the Acquired Entity's ability relative to comparable companies;
   (iv) effects arising from force majeure events, including natural disasters or epidemic outbreaks, unless they uniquely and adversely affect the Acquired Entity's consummation timeline; and
   (v) any effects resulting from the public disclosure or announcement of this Agreement.
The key emphasis in this definition is that while subclause (2) expressly refers to impacts on the Acquired Entity's capability to consummate the Acquisition, the list of carveouts in subclause (1) does not extend to subclause (2), thus implying that any factor adversely affecting the ability to consummate is not shielded by these exceptions.
