Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to the Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts provided below?

Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
“Material Adverse Effect” means any adverse change, event, or condition that, either individually or in combination, (a) would prevent, materially delay, or impair the ability of the Company to consummate the Transactions, or (b) would cause a material adverse impact on the financial condition, operations, assets, or prospects of the Company. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following exceptions shall not be taken into account when assessing a Material Adverse Effect under clause (b): 
   (i) general economic downturns or shifts in the global economy; 
   (ii) fluctuations in credit, capital, or securities markets, including changes in interest or exchange rates; 
   (iii) changes in GAAP, regulatory frameworks, or applicable Law; 
   (iv) acts of nature such as earthquakes, hurricanes, or other natural disasters; 
   (v) any adverse effects arising solely from public announcement or rumor of this Agreement; and 
   (vi) operational disruptions affecting only routine business practices.
However, it is expressly provided that the exceptions listed above under clauses (i) through (vi) do not apply to any event that impairs, delays, or prevents the ability of the Company to consummate the Transactions. In such instances, the consummation provision shall be evaluated independently and is not subject to any of the carveouts noted herein.

End of Excerpt.
